The user is asking about the return value of the `session_checkpoint` function when free GPU memory is insufficient.

Looking at the code:

```rust
pub fn session_checkpoint(
    &self,
    cache: &EngineCache,
) -> Result<Option<EngineCache>, ModelError> {
    match (self, cache) {
        (Self::Qwen35Cuda(m), EngineCache::Qwen35Cuda(kv)) => {
            let free = u64::try_from(m.gpu().info()?.free_bytes).unwrap_or(0);
            let copy = u64::try_from(kv.checkpoint_bytes()).unwrap_or(u64::MAX);
            if free < copy.saturating_add(GPU_SLACK_BYTES) {
                return Ok(None);
            }
            Ok(Some(EngineCache::Qwen35Cuda(Box::new(
                m.checkpoint_cache(kv)?,
            ))))
        }
        (Self::Qwen35Cuda(_), _) => Err(wrong_backend()),
        _ => Ok(None),
    }
}
```

The function checks if `free < copy.saturating_add(GPU_SLACK_BYTES)`. If this condition is true (meaning free memory is below the checkpoint size plus the slack bytes), it returns `Ok(None)`.

So the answer is: `Ok(None)`
</think>

When free GPU memory is below the checkpoint size plus `GPU_SLACK_BYTES`, the `session_checkpoint` function returns `Ok(None)`.

This is explicitly handled in the code:

```rust
if free < copy.saturating_add(GPU_SLACK_BYTES) {
    return Ok(None);
}
```

The function returns `None` to indicate that a checkpoint cannot be created due to insufficient memory, while still succeeding with `Ok(None)` rather than failing with an error.
